+XHCI Debug Capability
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+
+The xHCI Debug Capability (DbC, xHCI 1.2 section 7.6) lets a host
+controller present one of its ports as a USB device to another host, the
+debug host, over a USB 3 debug cable. Linux uses it for ``ttyDBC`` and
+for early console output. With ``dbc=on``, ``qemu-xhci`` offers the
+capability to its guest:
+
+.. parsed-literal::
+
+    |qemu_system| -device qemu-xhci,id=target,dbc=on \\
+                  -device qemu-xhci,id=debughost \\
+                  -device usb-dbc-peer,dbc=target,bus=debughost.0
+
+``usb-dbc-peer`` is the cable's other end: a USB device, on any USB bus,
+here on a second controller standing in for the debug host. Its ``dbc``
+property names the controller whose Debug Capability it plugs into, and
+each such controller takes one cable. The device is attached only while
+the guest has DbC enabled. It enumerates with the vendor, product and
+strings the guest programmed, with one interface and a bulk endpoint in
+each direction.
+
+Migration keeps the Debug Capability's state, so DbC stays enabled in
+the guest. The debug host's controller does not migrate the transfers it
+had in flight to the cable, though, so after a migration the cable is
+unplugged and plugged back in, and both ends see a reconnect.
+
 
